WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.257
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:09.222
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.522 --> 00:00:15.727
Unit eleven: Market Analysis. Today you will make projections with estimate / forecast language.

4
00:00:16.027 --> 00:00:18.842
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.998
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.188
Market research.

7
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:31.573
Market research means the collection and analysis of information about customers and competitors.

8
00:00:32.183 --> 00:00:35.472
Market research showed strong demand for halal snacks.

9
00:00:36.083 --> 00:00:39.061
Your turn. Say: Market research.

10
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.888
Market research.

11
00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:46.243
Total addressable market.

12
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:52.512
Total addressable market means the total revenue available if a product captured the whole market.

13
00:00:53.117 --> 00:00:58.471
The total addressable market for e-bikes in Cambodia is estimated at $40 million.

14
00:00:59.083 --> 00:01:02.107
Your turn. Say: Total addressable market.

15
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:06.595
Total addressable market.

16
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:08.908
Competitive landscape.

17
00:01:09.917 --> 00:01:13.913
Competitive landscape means the overall picture of rivals in a market.

18
00:01:14.517 --> 00:01:17.782
The competitive landscape changed when a Thai chain arrived.

19
00:01:18.383 --> 00:01:21.454
Your turn. Say: Competitive landscape.

20
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.951
Competitive landscape.

21
00:01:26.717 --> 00:01:28.147
Demand forecasting.

22
00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:32.682
Demand forecasting means predicting future customer demand.

23
00:01:33.283 --> 00:01:36.560
Demand forecasting prevented overstock after the festival.

24
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:40.615
Your turn. Say: Demand forecasting.

25
00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:44.979
Demand forecasting.

26
00:01:45.717 --> 00:01:46.845
Market trend.

27
00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:51.266
Market trend means a general direction in which a market is moving.

28
00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.173
A clear market trend is the shift to cashless payments.

29
00:01:55.783 --> 00:01:58.981
Your turn. Say: Market trend.

30
00:02:01.833 --> 00:02:02.939
Market trend.

31
00:02:03.683 --> 00:02:04.998
Customer segment.

32
00:02:05.983 --> 00:02:10.375
Customer segment means a group of customers with similar characteristics.

33
00:02:10.983 --> 00:02:14.899
University students are the fastest-growing customer segment.

34
00:02:15.483 --> 00:02:18.252
Your turn. Say: Customer segment.

35
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.357
Customer segment.

36
00:02:23.117 --> 00:02:24.420
Barrier to entry.

37
00:02:25.417 --> 00:02:29.889
Barrier to entry means an obstacle that makes it hard for new firms to enter a market.

38
00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:34.108
High licensing costs are a barrier to entry in banking.

39
00:02:34.717 --> 00:02:37.671
Your turn. Say: Barrier to entry.

40
00:02:40.533 --> 00:02:41.813
Barrier to entry.

41
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:43.656
Market share.

42
00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:48.739
Market share means a company's percentage of total sales in a market.

43
00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.511
They hold roughly 30% market share in Phnom Penh.

44
00:02:53.117 --> 00:02:55.943
Your turn. Say: Market share.

45
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.022
Market share.

46
00:03:00.833 --> 00:03:04.623
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:05.483 --> 00:03:11.003
Visal, you claim the market for electric motorbikes in Cambodia will triple. Isn't that optimistic?

48
00:03:12.117 --> 00:03:24.103
It's based on data. Analysts forecast demand to grow by around 25% a year as fuel prices rise and charging stations spread.

49
00:03:25.217 --> 00:03:27.228
But forecasts are often wrong.

50
00:03:28.317 --> 00:03:38.167
They are, which is why we present a range. We estimate the market at between thirty and fifty million dollars, depending on government incentives.

51
00:03:39.283 --> 00:03:41.074
And what about competitors?

52
00:03:42.183 --> 00:03:51.395
The main barrier to entry is the charging network. It is projected that whoever builds it first will capture most of the market share.

53
00:03:52.533 --> 00:03:54.208
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:58.245
Grammar focus: Estimate and forecast language.

55
00:03:58.545 --> 00:03:59.790
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:00.483 --> 00:04:03.365
Analysts forecast demand based on market trends.

57
00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:07.852
We estimate the market at between $30 and $50 million.

58
00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:12.085
It is projected that sales will double by 2030.

59
00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:22.419
Hedge forecasts responsibly: 'is expected to', 'is projected to', 'roughly', 'an estimated'. Use 'grow by' for the amount of change and 'grow to' for the final figure.

60
00:04:23.183 --> 00:04:28.204
Be careful! Don't say: Analysts forecast demand to grow of 5%.

61
00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:33.734
Say: Analysts forecast demand to grow by 5%.

62
00:04:34.034 --> 00:04:35.418
Grow by + amount.

63
00:04:36.233 --> 00:04:38.944
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:39.733 --> 00:04:41.129
Sizing Up a Market.

65
00:04:41.429 --> 00:04:47.864
Market analysis helps a business decide whether an opportunity is large and accessible enough to justify investment.

66
00:04:48.867 --> 00:04:55.615
Analysts typically estimate the total addressable market first, then narrow it to the segment the company can realistically serve.

67
00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:04.451
Demand is forecast using historical data, surveys and observable trends, although every forecast rests on assumptions that may prove wrong.

68
00:05:05.467 --> 00:05:12.145
Equally important is the competitive landscape, since high barriers to entry can protect existing firms from newcomers.

69
00:05:13.133 --> 00:05:18.732
Responsible analysts therefore present ranges and scenarios rather than a single confident number.

70
00:05:19.833 --> 00:05:23.928
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:24.783 --> 00:05:27.747
Sales are projected blank by 10% next year.

72
00:05:28.047 --> 00:05:31.224
A, growing. B, to grow. C, grow.

73
00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:36.488
The answer is B: to grow.

74
00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:41.298
An obstacle that makes it hard for new firms to enter is a blank.

75
00:05:41.598 --> 00:05:46.668
A, market trend. B, barrier to entry. C, customer segment.

76
00:05:50.233 --> 00:05:53.046
The answer is B: barrier to entry.

77
00:05:54.183 --> 00:05:57.808
A company's percentage of total sales in a market is its blank.

78
00:05:58.108 --> 00:06:03.317
A, market share. B, market research. C, demand forecasting.

79
00:06:06.867 --> 00:06:09.470
The answer is A: market share.

80
00:06:10.617 --> 00:06:12.489
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:12.789 --> 00:06:21.574
A, The market is estimated at $5 million.. B, The market is estimated in $5 million.. C, The market estimates at $5 million..

82
00:06:25.133 --> 00:06:28.665
The answer is A: The market is estimated at $5 million..

83
00:06:29.817 --> 00:06:33.082
According to the reading, responsible analysts present…

84
00:06:33.382 --> 00:06:39.380
A, one exact number. B, ranges and scenarios. C, only past data.

85
00:06:42.933 --> 00:06:45.873
The answer is B: ranges and scenarios.

86
00:06:47.067 --> 00:06:50.450
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:51.317 --> 00:06:54.837
What is one barrier to entry a new business might face in a market?

88
00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:13.150
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:52.264
One major barrier to entry is the high cost of building trust and distribution. In Cambodia's banking sector, for example, a new bank must obtain an expensive licence, open branches and convince customers to move their savings. Established banks already enjoy strong brands and large networks, so it is estimated that newcomers need several years to break even. However, technology can lower some barriers, as digital wallets have shown. I think the toughest barrier is usually customer habit rather than money.

90
00:07:52.564 --> 00:07:54.436
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:59.813
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and estimate and forecast language.

92
00:08:00.113 --> 00:08:04.925
Today's value is analytical thinking. Look for the pattern underneath the surface.

93
00:08:05.225 --> 00:08:07.889
See you in Unit twelve. Goodbye!

94
00:08:08.189 --> 00:08:10.900
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

